# `golem` (runtime)

`SetPhysicsMotionPolicy` uses optional backend atomic policy mutation without detaching entities or replacing handles. Require explicit retain/clear velocity, complete compatible controller config and navigation mode compatibility; pending frames/callbacks/restoration cannot mutate. Success synchronizes bound state and invalidates old trajectory tokens without rewinding the world. `QueuePhysicsMotionPolicy` defers contact-callback requests to the next tick and rejects stale attachment/policy generations as no-ops. Rejected backend admission leaves the old intent and bindings intact. Policy/controller intent lives in physical checkpoints; transient queue/token generations do not.

`ActivatePhysicsGeometry` is the activation-first path: after complete tick-side quarantine, activate already prepared physics source, then schedule asynchronous navigation preparation. The new physical state remains defined and quarantined after navigation failure or queue rejection; stable pending metadata survives for retry. Repeating the exact identity retries navigation without recreating physical bodies. Superseded identities cannot publish, including when a newer queue admission fails. The fully private `QueuePhysicsGeometry` path remains available separately.

Pending-world checkpoints retain accepted physical state/source leases, an explicit accepted navigation capture, desired physics/navigation bytes and stable activated/private change metadata separately. Capture fails closed while geometry is pending unless accepted-world captures are supplied. Pending restore requires `PreparePendingWorld` to privately build one `PreparedPhysicsRecovery` matching the complete checkpoint/desired fingerprints and revisions, including desired source lease ownership. It must migrate dynamic state using stable IDs and cannot mutate the live world. Root validates both candidates before publication, invalidates all pre-restore worker generations, and opens readiness only after the pair and entities/intents commit. Ordinary restore cannot rebuild newer desired navigation against older accepted physics.

Physical attachment and teleport admission require optional backend `PlacementValidator` capability. Synchronize before reading the current body/admitting its complete solid volume, and validate before owner, pose or entity writes. Default geometric penetration tolerance is zero; only explicit `PhysicsPlacementPolicy` permits overlap. This policy is independent of solver slop. ExternalPose bodies use the same admitted anchor path. A solver's rejected motion still returns actual accepted displacement from interactions; discard proposed navigation/traversal progress, retain actual support where valid and report Blocked/Unsupported without rolling bodies back.

**Paired physics recovery:** `CapturePhysicsCheckpoint` owns authoritative entity bytes, command owners, body bindings, source leases, desired physics/navigation captures and every navigation attachment/intent under one ID. Capture on the tick outside frames/callbacks after queued commands drain. `SavePhysicsCheckpoint` is worker-owned filesystem work: immutable content-addressed data first, manifest last. Recovery privately reconstructs fresh generational physics handles and validates entity/binding/source ownership before publication. Hosts must quiesce gameplay, disconnect clients and stop accepting new connections throughout restore. A failure after navigation publication keeps readiness closed for a complete retry; never expose mixed state. Registry replacement suppresses ordinary spawn/remove hooks, and `OnPhysicsRestored` runs only after all bindings and navigation intents are ready. Ephemeral visibility, interest, avatar/session and replication membership is reset and must be rebuilt explicitly. Runtime routes, contact caches and solver scratch are not persistent identities.

**Prepared physical world changes:** `QueuePhysicsGeometry` uses the bounded navigation build queue and exact key/revision/fingerprint pairing. Its tick-side callback must quarantine the full old/new physical volume and navigation bake/clearance influence before workers prepare source. Regional safety's negative bounds result confirms an intersecting barrier, not proof of complete coverage: the host owns that quarantine obligation. Publication validates current dynamic-body bounds and source ownership, loads candidate physics, atomically commits navigation, then removes the old lease. Before-publication failures remove only the candidate; unexpected cleanup failure faults readiness for paired recovery. Unaffected bodies retain handles and continue through the ordinary regional scheduler. Attached entity bodies must detach before their source lease is replaced.

`ObservePhysicsFrames` reports each attempted frame, accepted simulated duration/substeps, total/solve host duration, backend counts and errors. Actual `Intervals`/`Microsteps` and targeted `CCDCandidatePairs`/`CCDPairTests`/`CCDPredictiveContacts` survive failed attempts; predictive solver manifolds are not contact callback events. The retired global-guard counters remain separate and zero for native stepping. Its read-only callback is outside measured duration. Before sealing staged navigation, discard superseded plans and recapture safety after discard callbacks without rerunning fair scheduling; unaffected valid proposals continue.

The Box3D adapter's default physical cadence is four native steps within one 20 Hz server frame, with one velocity and one relaxation pass. Gameplay boundaries may partition shared time but may not repeat navigation fairness, multiply force duration or add a second integrator. Root depends only on portable contracts. Captured navigation/trajectory safety still validates every observed actual path and rejects the whole interacting frame on unexpected unsafe motion; normal physical contact compliance does not introduce a universal penetration-depth failure gate. World CCD and selective body bullet policy are distinct backend settings, never inferred from root navigation attachment.

Paired checkpoint envelopes retain version 1 while embedded portable physics uses version 2 and native solver settings. `DecodePhysicsCheckpoint` must report obsolete physical payloads before unknown retired configuration fields obscure the migration error. An explicit state migration is required; ordinary entity snapshots retain their existing format.

**Surface navigation:** `SetSurfaceNavigation` is startup-only and depends on `nav/surface`, never navmesh-go. `AttachNavigation` requires a registered `Spatial3DEntity` + `Position3DWriter`; `DeleteEntity` detaches before `OnRemove`. Intent and build APIs are tick-owned (startup is allowed where documented); `NavigationStats` alone reads queue counters concurrently. Independent path/build pools have bounded pending/completion queues, stable-key coalescing, priority aging, cancellation and generation checks. Workers capture values, own query scratch, and retain immutable views; they never read live entities. Complete build publication drains before path results after session messages. Managed movement runs after `OnTick`, before collision; actual-body reconciliation runs after 3D readback and before replication. Pool shutdown cancels/joins workers and discards unfinished results without publishing them.

`RegionalBackend` permits movement outside dirty tile columns while desired geometry is pending. Preserve quarantined support/destinations and wait for a changed safety token; do not busy-resubmit paths. Missing required profiles, restoration and restoration errors remain global barriers. A committed regional snapshot with pending changes is Ready/Degraded. Backends exposing only `PendingBackend` retain profile-wide pauses. Rebase unchanged support/corridors into the latest view within the fair bounded movement pass, swap all handles atomically, then release the old view. Search queries stay job-owned; following agents receive fresh lightweight queries. Initial bounded projection aligns the body through its motor; later reconciliation, retained-support replanning and traversal landings enforce `SupportTolerance` (default 0.05 world units) as a full body-to-surface distance bound. Configure tolerance against bake/body error below distinct-floor separation; do not silently snap established bodies across missing support. Steering and arrival use projected surface coordinates, while motor input, body writes, velocity and stall detection use physical coordinates. Verify attachment/generation and fresh regional safety after callbacks; check full movement segments and collision readback, not only endpoints. Float64 navigation results must fit Golem's float32 entity positions. Regional coordinate management belongs to the host.

`NavigationMotor` resolves body movement; a nil motor is navmesh-only motion. The built-in 3D overlap detector is not a character motor. `NavigationTraversal` begins a game-owned directed-link action, which advances separately, validates landing and is cancelled on interruption/removal. Ordinary moving agents do not rebuild geometry. `NavigationCheckpoint` contains stable intent identities and opaque desired-world bytes; hosts capture matching entity bytes under one checkpoint ID and commit their manifest last. Runtime locations/routes are never persistent identities.

**Staged physical navigation:** `NavigationAgentConfig.StagedPhysics` opts into `PrepareNavigationFrame` / `CommitNavigationFrame` / `AbortNavigationFrame`; it cannot combine with the synchronous motor. Prepare executes the existing fair scheduler once per server tick. Retained ordered motion segments apportion that frame's actual time; skipped scheduling must not increase simulation time. Zero-time initial projection is an explicit clearance-validated admission request. Proposed support and corner progress stay private until all body results validate. Captured safety covers every staged attachment, including idle bodies pushed by others, full paths and traversal bounds. `NavigationFrame.Frozen` requires a pre-solver stationary constraint during quarantine/readiness barriers. Unexpected unsafe physical paths fault the whole interacting frame; never roll back one character after it transferred impulses. Valid displacement that loses surface support remains the actual pose and reports Unsupported. `NavigationStagedTraversalAction` separates Prepare from exactly one Commit/Discard; all navigation support is reconciled before committed traversal callbacks run. Legacy synchronous navigation retains its existing behavior and later collision reconciliation.

**Physics ownership:** `SetPhysicsWorld` installs the independent `physics3d.World` before Run and transfers shutdown ownership. Root runtime imports portable contracts, never Box3D. `AttachPhysics` requires a registered 3D reader/writer, an already matching gameplay anchor and no legacy collider provider or synchronous navigation authority. Attachment owns body/collider/joint destruction on detach/delete. `PhysicsAttachmentConfig` explicitly opts into rotation, COM linear/angular velocity and activation writers; position always reads the gameplay anchor, not body origin or COM. Writers are data-only. `TeleportPhysics` preserves the body-local anchor and synchronizes query/entity visibility. `QueuePhysicsCommand` is bounded and tick-owned; callbacks queue mutations/deletions for a later tick and cannot step or directly mutate the world. The runtime validates every body and navigation path, accepts the world, writes all bindings, reconciles all support, then dispatches copied contact events. A rejected frame is never replicated; accepted callback panics follow ordinary server shutdown semantics without individual-body rollback. Physics-bound entities are skipped by legacy collision position updates/readback. Startup or tick callbacks may attach; physics event callbacks defer ownership changes. Shutdown rejects new commands, discards queued mutations and closes the owned world; optional `ShutdownWorld` supplies immutable terminal events for accepted pairs only.

Optional `NavigationBoundedTraversal` declares the complete fixed swept volume of an action; validate endpoints and every returned position against it. Unknown traversal bounds cause conservative cancellation on profile safety changes. Hosts must establish navigation quarantine before activating/removing physical geometry. `NavigationConfig.ObserveTick` observes prepare/movement/reconciliation durations and scheduled-agent counts after reconciliation; its observer must not mutate server state. Qualification must report cadence alongside timing so time budgets cannot conceal overload.

Cache successful route safety only for the same immutable route and safety token; invalidate it on release, path admission, repair and rebase. Cache location safety only for the same immutable location and safety token; clear it on every location assignment and view transfer. Recapture safety and check full physical movement segments at each acceptance boundary, including after callbacks. Quarantined agents resume acceleration from rest; unchanged distant rebases preserve their velocity.

Successful point/segment safety may be reused only for the exact same coordinates/directed endpoints and immutable safety token. Always recapture after callbacks; clear these caches on view transfer/release. `AllowPartial` corridors can rebase successfully with a Partial result: retain that status through normal following, quarantine resumption and asynchronous admission so the reachable prefix never reports the original destination as reached.

For `RetainedRegionalSafety` with `ProtectsRetiredViews`, use the independent fair retirement cursor and `RebaseAgentsPerTick`/`RebaseBudget` (64 agents/1 ms by default). Unaffected old views may continue moving only because backend checks quarantine every change since all still-leased retired generations, including full motor/traversal/collision segments. Quarantined agents still participate in retirement; failed support transfers must release their old view so quarantine can drain. Never defer rebasing with ordinary `RegionalSafety` alone: its segment check need not protect changes already published. `HasQuarantine` keeps readiness degraded during retirement even when committed and desired revisions match. Callback-induced restoration stops the remaining movement/retirement passes immediately.

`ViewRegionalSafety.ForView` narrows retirement quarantine to the agent's live generation; pending/unloaded columns still apply to every generation. Select it after every fresh capture and view transfer, including motor/traversal/collision acceptance. Never use current-view segment safety for an older view. Clear handle caches on transfer, and recheck quarantine even when the broader publication token has not changed. Backends without scoped captures retain conservative union behavior.

`ObservePathAdmission` timestamps only a successful validated worker result entering Following, including accepted partial routes. Keep unsafe completed results as owned pending routes so token changes can revalidate the actual corridor without another search. Emit admission once, only after safety clears; discard pending admission on cancellation, replacement or invalidation. It does not report failures or ordinary rebases. Consumers pair the entity/intent generation with their own request timestamp and separately record failed, cancelled, superseded and unresolved requests. Its read-only observer execution is excluded from the containing navigation phase timing but remains in the full tick duration.

Use `NavigationNow` and same-domain `Time.Sub`/`navigationSince` for navigation budgets and latency measurements. Windows uses QPC anchored to one process-local `time.Time` value; ordinary `time.Now`/`time.Since` there can quantize sub-millisecond work to zero and overrun short budgets. Never mix QPC-anchored timestamps with `time.Since` or `time.Until`. The anchored wall representation is for local interval evidence, not synchronization across processes or later civil-clock corrections. Production builds remain `CGO_ENABLED=0`; the Windows helper calls the operating system directly.

`surface.SupportIdentity` permits retaining the funnel/corner cursor during straight progress inside the same convex support polygon. A polygon change or any lateral move/motor/collision deviation requires ordinary corridor repair; compare lateral positions at entity float32 precision, not an accumulating distance tolerance. Reuse a previous physical-position projection when unchanged, or retain the exact surface returned by Move when body XZ matches at entity precision and full body-to-support distance remains within tolerance. Fresh regional safety checks still apply. Clear every retained support/projection cache on route release or rebase. Traversal collision readback also checks quarantine and declared bounds, restoring the last accepted position when safe to do so under the same intent generation.

Use optional `CorridorTransferQuery` during view transfer only for an existing route with reconciled body position, unchanged convex support since the last repair, no initial projection, no active traversal and no pending lateral deviation. It preserves the whole corridor, corner order and cursor; a transfer rejection cannot be treated as repair success. Otherwise use `RebaseQuery` repair and accept any Partial result only under the agent's partial-route policy. Swap view/query/support/route together before releasing the old lease, and invalidate all safety caches for the new scope.

**Game loop + integrated networking + world data + interest management:** `Server`, `ServerConfig` (with `Addr`, `Path`, `StaticDir`, `Transport`, `StateUpdateLane`, `CellSize` for FOI, optional `LogReplicationStats` (or env `GOLEM_LOG_REPLICATION_STATS=1`) for once-per-second replication batching / wire-size diagnostics), `TickFunc` `func(dt float64, s *Server)`, `ContactFunc` `func(contacts []CollisionContact)`, `OnTick` / `OnUpdates` / `OnContact`, `SetRemovalSerializer`, `SetCollisionBackend`, `Run` with fixed timestep. **Entity world API on `Server`:** `CreateEntity(e, owner ...int64) error` (unowned vs owned via optional session ID), `DeleteEntity`, `Get`, `Owner`, `SetOwner`, `All`, `Len`, `SnapshotAll`—all delegate to an internal `*registry.Registry` (`reg` field, unexported). Optional **`ServerBinder`** (`BindServer(*Server)`): `CreateEntity` calls it after ID assignment and before registry insertion, so `OnSpawn` can use a bound server; snapshot restore goes through `CreateEntity` and gets the same binding. CreateEntity uses `AddWithoutSpawn`/`AddOwnedWithoutSpawn` then collider registration then `registry.NotifySpawn` so spawn hooks observe bound server and registered colliders. **Session avatars:** `SpawnAvatar(sess, e, AvatarOptions{FOIRadius, FOIMargin})` does `CreateEntity(e, sess.ID)`, assigns FOI when `FOIRadius > 0` (errors if interest is disabled or radius is negative; `0` disables FOI), and records concurrency-safe `sessionID↔entityID` indexes under `avatarMu` (not held across registry hooks or interest ops). FOI goes through `AssignFOI`/`RemoveFOI` (`interestMu`). Lock order when both are needed: `interestMu` before `avatarMu`. `Avatar(sessionID)` and generic `AvatarOf[T](s, sessionID)` look up the bound entity. Duplicate `SpawnAvatar` for a live avatar is rejected (no replace). On disconnect, user `OnDisconnect` runs first (avatar still readable; listener always delivers a non-nil `*Session`), then automatic `RemoveFOI` (when interest enabled) and reaping of any avatar still bound to the session — including a replacement spawned inside the callback — so avatars cannot leak past disconnect. Avatar rollback and disconnect use `Server.DeleteEntity`, removing registered 2D and 3D colliders independently before `OnRemove`. Mid-session `DeleteEntity` clears matching avatar indexes in O(1); unlinking only removes matching bidirectional entries, leaving pending reservations to the reservation helpers. `SetOwner` changes command authority only — it does not transfer avatar identity. `NewServer` always creates an internal `golem/net.Listener` wired to that registry, with `WrapEntityUpdate` as the reliable stream entity message wrapper and a world snapshot closure. Automatic incremental entity replication uses `StateUpdateLaneDatagram` per session by default: WebTransport receives compact eventual-state datagrams while WebSocket fallback sessions receive equivalent wrapped deltas on the reliable stream. `StateUpdateLaneStream` keeps incremental updates on the reliable stream for every session. Datagram state updates fall oversized/full-state payloads back to the reliable stream. Reliable-stream chunk statistics use each session's actual transport. When `CellSize > 0`, an `interest.Manager` is created and interest management is enabled (entity snapshots on connect are deferred). When `Addr` is set, `Run` starts both the tick loop and the built-in HTTP server plus the selected transport endpoint (WebTransport by default, WebSocket when configured with `StateUpdateLaneStream`). When `Addr` is empty, `Run` only ticks; the game mounts `Handler()` on its own router or HTTP/3 server and may mount `WebSocketHandler()` on a separate HTTP server. `Run` shutdown closes sessions accepted through either handler.

**Interest management:** when enabled (`CellSize > 0`), `AssignFOI(sessionID, entityID, radius, margin)` and `RemoveFOI(sessionID)` control per-session fields of interest. All Server FOI / visibility / broadcast-known access (`AssignFOI`, `RemoveFOI`, visibility group APIs, `SessionsKnowing` known-set reads, and the `UpdateGrid`+`ComputeDiffsFiltered` critical section) is guarded by `interestMu` so gameplay can call these APIs off the tick goroutine; `interestMu` is not held across network sends or registry hooks. `runInterestTick` copies filtered diffs under the mutex, then flushes and sends unlocked. Entered entities get `FullUpdate`, stayed+dirty get delta or compact datagram record, exited get removal. Global entities (`IsGlobal() == true`) bypass distance but still honor visibility-group membership. Sessions without FOI still receive no entities (including grouped globals). When interest is **not** enabled, `runBroadcastTick` uses per-session known-set diffs when visibility groups (or known-set reconciliation) require it, and keeps a blind fast path that wraps once and calls `SendBatch` per session when every session already knows the live public set and neither owner-scoped entities nor ownership refreshes require filtering.

**Replication implementation:** `loop.go` owns orchestration and known-set policy. `replication.go` shares filtered/interest flush preparation, immutable payload caches, per-recipient delivery, removal serialization, and stream submission counting. Interest captures copied diffs and ownership refreshes before `FlushAll`; filtered broadcast captures recipients/prepared eventual frames before `OnUpdates`, then computes live diffs and captures refreshes after it. Keep those timing differences and the blind fast path separate. Actual deletions use registry removal revisions; visibility/FOI exits use the current revision (zero when absent). `owner_replication.go` owns recipient view selection with separate authoritative/public caches. `eventual_state.go` owns dirty merging and one packing/delivery loop for cached or prepared frames; successful owned datagram submission transfers its buffer, while tracker descriptors and future packet buffers remain independent. Production replication reuses `golem/net` framing/budget helpers; it does not own wire protocol or ACK scheduling.

**Visibility groups:** `Server` always owns one `golem/visibility.Manager` (no policy mirroring into interest). Public APIs: `JoinVisibilityGroup`, `LeaveVisibilityGroup`, `SetEntityVisibilityGroup` (empty group = public). Concurrency-safe under `interestMu` and never wait on network I/O. Policy is point-in-time: blind-vs-filtered decisions and connect-snapshot allowed-ID selection linearize under `interestMu`, then the mutex is released before `FullUpdate` / `SendBatch` / writes. A mutation after that decision takes effect on the next replication pass via known-set enter/exit (already selected/queued frames are not retroactively revoked). `SessionsKnowing` consults actual known sets in both modes, never raw membership. Broadcast known state is cleared if SendBatch observes a disconnected session after enter decisions. Group/tombstone metadata for deleted entities is retained until that tick's removal decisions are handled, then cleared. Disconnect clears session membership and broadcast known state (FOI cleanup remains in avatar disconnect).

**Owner-only vars:** entities implementing `OwnerScopedEntity` (generated when YAML has `visibility: owner`) send authoritative full/delta to the current owner and `PublicFullUpdate` / `PublicReplicationMask`-filtered deltas to everyone else (zero public mask → no delta). Blind broadcast is disabled when any live entity is owner-scoped or an ownership refresh is pending. Connect snapshots use the same owner-vs-public selection after visibility-group filtering. `SetOwner` updates command authority immediately; Owner lookup, registry mutation, and refresh coalescing are atomic under `interestMu` (before registry lock) relative to other `Server.SetOwner` calls, and locks are never held across sends/hooks. It queues a coalesced replication refresh for owner-scoped entities (original old known owner → public full; final new known owner → authoritative full; same-tick A→…→A cancels; `0` clears ownership). Refresh is gated by known-set stay (entered already got correct full; exited get removal) and clears that session/entity eventual tracker so pending private deltas cannot survive a full clear/grant. Public eventual frames reapply `PublicReplicationMask` at serialize time (defense in depth against stale private bits after loss/requeue merge). Eventual-state cache keys include a public bit so private bytes are never reused for non-owners. Persistence/`SnapshotAll` stay authoritative. `visibility: owner` is replication redaction, not command authorization.

**Collision integration:** opt-in via `SetCollisionBackend(b collision.Backend)` / `SetCollision3DBackend`. When a backend is set, each tick runs after `onTick` and before the flush: (1) `backend.Update` for every live entity, (2) `backend.Step(dt)` → contacts, (3) `backend.ReadBack` writes physics-corrected positions back to `PositionWriter` / 3D writers, (4) `onContact` / `onContact3D` when contacts were found, (5) 2D-only `dispatchContactEvents` when enabled (pre-existing; not part of schema-collider 3D parity). **Named layers:** `CollisionLayers` (2D) and `CollisionLayers3D` (3D) with `Bind`/`Define`/`SetCollides`/`Add`/`Set`/`Remove`/`Layer`/`Mask`/`MaskFor`; store via `SetCollisionLayers` / `SetCollisionLayers3D`. Generated `EnableCollision`/`EnableCollision3D` call `MustCollisionBackend`/`MustCollisionBackend3D` so nil backends panic before installing helpers. **Automatic schema colliders:** `ColliderProvider` and `ColliderProvider3D` register/unregister independently when both helpers are set; `CreateEntity` (including snapshot restore) registers after successful `AddWithoutSpawn` and before `NotifySpawn`/`OnSpawn`; `DeleteEntity` removes after avatar index cleanup and before registry deletion/`OnRemove`. Missing layer helpers skip registration without panicking; call EnableCollision helpers (or setters) before spawning/snapshot-loading collidable entities. Failed duplicate CreateEntity does not register a collider. **Spatial queries** (`spatial.go`): `OverlapBox`/`OverlapCircle`/`OverlapBox3D`/`OverlapSphere` (and cast helpers) return entity IDs from the collision backend; free function `OverlapOfType[T Entity](s, entityIDs)` resolves those IDs via `Server.Get`, retains only entities satisfying `T`, preserves input order, skips missing IDs, returns nil when input or result is empty, and always panics on nil `*Server` with `golem: OverlapOfType: Server must be non-nil` (including empty ID lists)—compose as `OverlapOfType[*Mob](s, s.OverlapCircle(...))` rather than adding geometry-specific generics.

**Per-entity collision events:** opt-in via `EnableContactEvents()`, which sets `contactEventsEnabled = true` and allocates `triggerPairs map[[2]int64]struct{}` and `solidPairs map[[2]int64]struct{}` (both fields on `Server`, defined in `loop.go`). Requires a collision backend. Six opt-in entity interfaces in `golem/collision_events.go`: `TriggerEnter`, `TriggerStay`, `TriggerExit` (method arg: `other Entity`); `CollisionEnter`, `CollisionStay`, `CollisionExit` (method args: `other Entity, normal CollisionVec2, depth float64`). `dispatchContactEvents(contacts []collision.Contact)` in `collision_events.go`: splits contacts into `currentTrigger` (Depth==0) and `currentSolid` (Depth!=0) maps keyed by `contactKey(a,b int64) [2]int64` (canonical min-first pair); fires Enter/Stay/Exit by comparing current maps against `triggerPairs`/`solidPairs`; swaps both maps only after trigger transitions (including exits) and then solid transitions finish. The shared pair traversal resolves both endpoints before either callback; typed emitters retain trigger/solid callback signatures. **Normal convention:** `Contact.Normal` is the push direction for `Contact.A` (unit vector from B toward A); entity B receives the negated vector so both sides get a normal pointing away from the other. **nil other:** if `reg.Get` returns false for a partner, `other` is passed as nil; the receiver entity still gets the callback (guarded by its own `okA`/`okB`). Both sides can be stale simultaneously — neither fires. `contactKey` returns `{min(a,b), max(a,b)}`.

Each tick order: **`s.tick++`** → **`OnTickStart(tick)`** → **`drainAsyncCallbacks()`** (bounded `SubmitTask` completions and `Server.Post` callbacks under one `AsyncCallbacksPerTick` budget; round-robin starts completion-first and persists across ticks) → **`drainMessages()`** (OnConnect / OnMessage / OnDisconnect) → optional navigation build publication and path results → **`reg.TickAll(dt)`** → `onTick(dt, s)` → optional navigation movement/motor → collision steps (Update → Step → ReadBack → contact callbacks/events) → optional navigation body reconciliation → flush + broadcast → **`OnTickEnd(tick, wall)`**. `tick` is a `uint64` field on `Server`, incremented at the top of each tick (1-based, single goroutine). `Tick()` returns it for game logic. Hook callbacks run on the tick goroutine so trace regions and profiling labels nest with simulation work.

**Bounded tasks / Post / Run lifecycle:** `Server.SubmitTask` runs work on an internal Pond v2 pool (`TaskWorkers` default 4, `TaskQueueCapacity` default 256, `pond.WithContext(runCtx)`); completions use an independent bounded queue (`TaskCompletionQueueCapacity` default 256). `Server.Post` enqueues a tick callback (never inline). Acceptance is gated by a linearizable `created → running → stopping → stopped` lifecycle under `lifeMu` (shared with non-blocking `postQueue` sends and `TrySubmitErr`). `Run` is single-use (`ErrServerAlreadyRun` on concurrent/repeated calls) and owns an independent run context + pool (caller ctx is not the Pond parent, so shutdown can cancel then `StopAndWait`). An already-cancelled caller `Run` context linearizes `stopping` under `lifeMu` before `runLoop` or Post/SubmitTask acceptance; live caller cancellation still links via `context.AfterFunc` after acceptance opens. `Post` / `SubmitTask` return `ErrServerNotRunning` before `Run`, once shutdown linearizes `stopping`, and after `Run` returns; full queues return `ErrPostQueueFull` / `ErrTaskQueueFull` without blocking. When the completion queue is full, finished workers select on enqueue vs run cancellation (backpressure, no drop while running). Effective work context is `WithCancel(callerCtx)` with run-context cancellation linked via AfterFunc (already-/queue-cancelled callers are visible synchronously when work starts); a non-nil work error is preserved, else a cancelled effective context surfaces as the completion error; panics are recovered in Golem's wrapper, logged with stack, and delivered as errors wrapping `ErrTaskPanicked` with exactly one tick completion while running. Ownership: workers must not touch Server/registry/visibility/session/tick-owned state — only completion/`Post` callbacks may. Config also includes `PostQueueCapacity` (default 1024) and `AsyncCallbacksPerTick` (default 256); zero means default, negative panics in `NewServer`. Shutdown order: stop accepting → cancel run/worker context → wait for Pond (queued-not-started tasks are not executed; running tasks receive cancellation) → discard queued completions/posts → stopped. No callback runs after the tick loop exits; work that ignores cancellation can delay `Run` return. **Observability:** `Server.TaskStats()` returns `TaskStats` with live gauges `QueuedTasks`/`RunningTasks` (Pond `WaitingTasks`/`RunningWorkers` when the pool exists) and `QueuedCompletions`/`QueuedPosts` (channel `len`; all four approximate under concurrency), plus Golem atomics `TasksAccepted`, `TasksFinished` (work return/panic only), `TaskCompletionsExecuted` (tick callback actually ran; shutdown discard keeps finished≠executed), `TasksRejectedFull`, `TasksCancelled` (queued-not-started discarded on shutdown, or work whose effective context ended — each accepted task at most once), `TaskPanics`, `PostsAccepted`, `PostsExecuted`, `PostsRejectedFull`. Do not mirror Pond submitted/dropped cumulatives: Pond counts full-queue drops in submitted. **Accepted-before-work:** each successful `TrySubmitErr` opens a per-submission start gate only after `TasksAccepted` increments under `lifeMu` (rejected submissions never open the gate), so finish/cancel/panic cannot race ahead of accepted; the tick is never blocked on queue capacity.

**Session event queue:** `OnConnect`, `OnMessage`, and `OnDisconnect` are enqueued from connection goroutines (via internal `enqueueConnect` / `enqueueMessage` / `enqueueDisconnect` hooks installed on the `Listener` in `NewServer`) and dispatched on the tick goroutine during `drainMessages`. The user-facing `OnConnect(fn)` / `OnMessage(fn)` / `OnDisconnect(fn)` methods **only store `fn`** — they no longer call `s.listener.On*`. This makes it safe to call `CreateEntity`, `DeleteEntity`, entity `Set*` methods, and all other `Server` APIs from all three callbacks without any additional synchronization. After the user `OnDisconnect` callback, the server runs avatar auto-cleanup (`RemoveFOI` / delete avatar entity / clear indexes). Events are queued in a buffered channel of capacity 4096; if the queue is full the event is dropped and a warning is logged (the connection goroutine is never blocked). Each event fires up to one tick after it occurs — `OnConnect` in particular fires on the tick after the world-state snapshot is delivered, which shifts when the player entity appears relative to the interest system's first visibility pass. `OnUpgrade` is unaffected and still runs synchronously before the WebSocket is accepted.

**External router support:** `Handler()` returns the configured-primary transport endpoint for mounting on a custom router (e.g. Chi alongside REST routes). `WebSocketHandler()` always returns a fallback handler backed by the same listener and game loop. `OnUpgrade(func(*http.Request) (any, error))` provides pre-upgrade auth; the returned value populates `Session.Data` before `OnConnect` fires, and a non-nil error rejects with HTTP 401. For token-in-query-param upgrades, use `golem/auth.UpgradeHandler` (stdlib-only helper; does not import `golem`). When using WebTransport on a caller-owned server, pair `Handler()` with `WebTransportServer(...)` on HTTP/3. Session lifecycle hooks: `OnConnect`, `OnMessage`, `OnDisconnect`, and `OnDatagram` for the lossy WebTransport lane. Direct-send helpers: `Send(sessionID, data)`, `Broadcast(data)`, `SendUnreliable(data)` / `BroadcastUnreliable(data)` when the target transport supports datagrams.

**Realtime bootstrap:** `RealtimeConfigHandler(RealtimeConfigOptions)` serves the primary `transport`/`url`, primary-only certificate hashes and eventual ACK interval, plus optional `fallback: {transport:"websocket",url}` from `WebSocketFallbackURL` when WebTransport is primary. Responses use `Cache-Control: no-store`; configuring a fallback for a non-WebTransport primary returns HTTP 500 rather than advertising an invalid chain.

**`servermsg.go`**: `WrapEntityUpdate` wraps `EntityUpdate` bytes in a `ServerMessage` envelope (proto field 1, length-delimited). `WrapWorldUpdate` wraps `WorldUpdate` bytes in a `ServerMessage` envelope (proto field 2, length-delimited). The `Listener` applies `WrapEntityUpdate` transparently via its `messageWrapper`; world frames bypass it entirely and use `WrapWorldUpdate` via the world snapshot closure or `BroadcastRaw`.

**`export.go`** re-exports `Entity`, opt-in entity interfaces `Ticker`, `Spawner`, `Remover`, `PositionWriter`, `Session` (from `golem/net`), `InterestManager`, `InterestFOI`, `InterestDiff` (from `golem/interest`), `WorldData`/`WorldStore` (from `golem/world`), and collision types including `CollisionBackend`, `CollisionShape`, `CollisionLayers`, `CollisionBackend3D`, `CollisionShape3D`, `CollisionLayers3D`, and related shape/contact aliases so consumers use a single **`golem`** import path. Six per-entity collision event interfaces (`TriggerEnter`, `TriggerStay`, `TriggerExit`, `CollisionEnter`, `CollisionStay`, `CollisionExit`) are defined directly in `golem/collision_events.go`. **`Registry` / `FlushResult` / `NewRegistry` are not re-exported**—use `golem.Server` for gameplay or import `golem/registry` for low-level embedding.

**World data** (`Server.World`): `*world.Store` is always initialized. `Server.PushWorldData(name) error` broadcasts one world type to all sessions; returns nil when the name is missing or networking is inactive, and returns a non-nil error if `MarshalUpdate` fails. `Server.SendWorldData(sessionID, data) error` is the primary per-session API: it marshals the supplied value directly (no `World.Set`), wraps with `WrapWorldUpdate`, and sends on the reliable stream to that session only — two sessions may receive different values with the same `WorldName`. Nil and typed-nil `data` return `golem: SendWorldData requires non-nil world data`. `Server.SendStoredWorldData(sessionID, name) error` matches `PushWorldData`'s missing-name nil/no-op, then delegates to `SendWorldData`. Marshal, oversize (256 KiB reliable frame cap), and disconnected-session errors propagate. `ServerConfig.WorldSnapshotExclude` lists names omitted from the connect snapshot; `NewServer` copies/normalizes the slice (drops empties/duplicates) into an immutable-after-construction exclude set used by the world snapshot closure. Excluded entries remain in `Server.World`; callers own on-demand delivery. Large embedded maps should use `map_url`. On connect, the listener still sends (non-excluded) world data before entity snapshots, in deterministic sorted name order.

**`golem/world`** package: `Data` interface (`WorldName`, `MarshalUpdate`), thread-safe `Store` (`Set`, `Get`, `MarshalAll` / `MarshalAllExcept(exclude)` in sorted name order). Pure data/serialization only — no session or network policy. No dependencies on `golem`, `golem/net`, or `golem/registry`.

Depends on **`golem/registry`** for entity storage, **`golem/net`** for networking, **`golem/interest`** for FOI management, **`golem/visibility`** for named group policy, **`golem/world`** for world data, and **`github.com/demiurgos-hub/golem-engine/golem/collision`** (local sibling module, resolved by `go.work`) for the collision backend interface; does **not** implement `Entity` or registry internals—those stay in `golem/registry/`.

**`golem/debug`** subpackage: `StartPprof(addr string) error` starts a pprof HTTP debug server on `addr` (e.g. `"127.0.0.1:6060"`). Uses a dedicated `http.ServeMux` — `http.DefaultServeMux` is not touched. All pprof handlers (`/debug/pprof/`, `/cmdline`, `/profile`, `/symbol`, `/trace`) are mounted explicitly via the named `net/http/pprof` import. Since the import lives only in `golem/debug`, production binaries that do not import it never register pprof handlers. Call from `main` behind a flag; never import in production builds.
